e one who knows who really killed  Roger Ackroyd and they're both wrong by  art is an inventive literary scholar  however as that indicates his most  recent book is called how to talk about  books you haven't read the title was  designed to get exactly that giggle from  you most of the reviews that have been  in journals and magazines are by people  who are giggling while they write the  review it's actually a very serious book  but one of the most interesting books of  literary criticism I read in a long time  about our reading practices the way some  of our reading practices consist of how  we have read a book that we read five  years ago or 30 years ago and we've read  it and we can talk about it and we know  about it but what we're remembering is  just a small subset of what we have  remembered and what we have done I found  myself I say getting off the train in  New Haven wishing the Pierre by art had  invented the Kindle rather than Jeff  Bezos and I think finally tools leave  traces and inventors leave traces on  their tools and I think that's probably  the last thing that I learned I've had  the suspicion for many years looking at  a variety of computer products that have  been developed at the great secret of  modern time is that Steve Jobs can't  type if Steve Jobs could type I argue  then his company would have produced far  fewer devices that are so maddeningly  frustrating to use for those of us who  have ten fingers and fewer people would  be stabbing touch screens with one  finger at a time trying to create words  out of doing that my discovery from this  device at the end of the day is that I  come away with a sneaking suspicion that  in the ways in which i've talked about  reading the forms of reading that i  think are possible and desirable the  forms of reading that this kind of  building as a temple of reading was  designed to facilitate and invent  foster and continue when you talk about  real reading I think it may make sense  at the end to say